---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Changelog" description="physure release history and version changes" activeSection="changelog">
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="kicker">Release History</div>
    <h1>Changelog</h1>
    <p>Version history and release notes for physure.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <p>physure is currently in <strong>Beta</strong>. The API may change between versions before the 1.0 stable release.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="releases">

    <div class="release current">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="unreleased">
          <span class="release-version">Unreleased</span>
          <span class="badge badge-green">In development</span>
          <span class="release-date">since v0.2.3</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <h3>Breaking</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>PHS local bindings moved to a postfix <code>where</code></strong>: <code>let name = value in body</code> is now <code>body where name = value[, name2 = value2]</code>. This freed the <code>in</code> keyword, so <code>5 in</code> is a length again.</li>
          <li><strong><code>physure.ext.grammar</code> removed</strong>: the Python reimplementation of the language (1655 lines) and its test module are gone. Use <a href="/api#grammar-api"><code>physure.Interpreter</code></a>, which runs the same Rust engine as <code>phs</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Added</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Vectors and matrices as first-class types</strong>: <a href="/api#tensors"><code>QuantityVector</code> and <code>QuantityMatrix</code></a> with dot, cross, norm, transpose, matmul and determinant — all propagating units, in Rust, PHS, Python and Java 8+.</li>
          <li><strong>N-dimensional native plotting</strong>, written in the crate with no new Rust dependency, plus <code>export3d(expr, "surface.stl")</code> writing a plotted surface as STL, OBJ, glTF or PLY.</li>
          <li><strong>String interpolation</strong>: <code>"the beam carries &#123;load&#125;"</code>, in the interpreter and all three transpile targets. A quoted string is now text, so a formula passed to <code>deriv</code> is no longer rewritten by whatever is in scope.</li>
          <li><strong>Asymmetric uncertainties as moments</strong>: <code>12.3 +/- (0.5, 0.4) pb</code> parses, and the pair maps onto the first three moments of a dimidiated Gaussian in closed form. Propagation deliberately raises rather than averaging the skew away.</li>
          <li><strong><code>physure.uncertainty_model(...)</code></strong>, a per-scope choice of distribution shape — <code>"gaussian"</code> today, <code>"moments"</code> once asymmetric propagation lands.</li>
          <li><strong><code>propagation_mode</code> is finally read from <code>physure.conf</code></strong>, by both PHS and the core, so the two cannot disagree. The key had been in the shipped conf from the beginning and nothing had ever read it.</li>
          <li><strong>PhyEquation & PhyFunction</strong>: equation arithmetic, callable equations, and transpilation parity tests across PHS, Rust, Python and Java 8+.</li>
          <li><strong>Advanced vector calculus</strong>: <code>diff(f, var, order)</code>, <code>grad</code>, <code>div</code>, <code>curl</code>, <code>laplacian</code>. Plus infinity (<code>inf</code>, <code>∞</code>, <code>oo</code>) in the grammar, limits and improper integrals.</li>
          <li><strong>Range syntax <code>a .. b</code></strong>, checked to be a real interval when built, and binding looser than the operators inside it.</li>
          <li><strong><code>%</code> and <code>ppm</code> are dimensionless units</strong>: <code>200 kPa * 5 %</code> is <code>10 kPa</code> — a unit-preserving operation the engine checks. Spanish aliases too: julio, vatio, pascalio, voltio, ohmio, porcentaje.</li>
          <li><strong>New format specs</strong>: <code>base</code> quotes a measurement in the units it is built from, <code>frac</code> and <code>ifrac</code> write the magnitude as a common or mixed fraction.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Fixed</h3>
        <p>Roughly thirty correctness fixes, several of which changed visible results:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Every prefixed unit was read one character short</strong> — <code>1 kg</code> evaluated as 1 gram, <code>100 kPa</code> as 100 pascal, <code>1 mol</code> as <code>1 * ol</code>.</li>
          <li><strong><code>1 km == 1000 m</code> was false</strong> while <code>1 km + 1000 m</code> converted correctly — comparison read the magnitudes and ignored the scale.</li>
          <li><strong>A quantity behaved as if independent of itself</strong>: <code>x - x</code> reported σ·√2 instead of zero, and <code>x / x</code> an uncertain 1. Python's scalar path now tracks provenance in the Rust core by default, matching PHS.</li>
          <li><strong>Java added uncertainties linearly, not in quadrature</strong> — 0.3 and 0.3 gave 0.6 where Rust and Python give 0.42, an error about 40% too large on every quantity through the Java path.</li>
          <li><strong><code>sin</code>, <code>cos</code>, <code>tan</code> are dimensionally checked</strong> and keep the uncertainty; <code>sin(9.81 m/s^2)</code> used to answer -0.379 as though m/s² were radians.</li>
          <li><strong>A degree is a plane angle again</strong> — <code>deg</code>, <code>arcmin</code> and <code>arcsec</code> had been registered as scaled steradians.</li>
          <li><strong>An unknown unit is reported instead of invented</strong>: <code>5 foobar</code> used to evaluate happily with a fabricated dimension.</li>
          <li><strong>A unit symbol the script also binds as a variable is rejected, not guessed</strong> — <code>g = 9.81 m/s^2</code> then <code>10.0 kg * g</code> read <code>g</code> as grams.</li>
          <li><strong><code>10.0 +/- 0.5 m</code> printed as <code>10.0 m</code></strong> — the uncertainty was propagated correctly and then dropped by <code>Display</code>.</li>
          <li><strong><code>QuantityVector::norm()</code> reported an uncertainty exactly √2 too small</strong>, and Monte Carlo propagation no longer redraws an operand that already carries samples.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The full list lives in the repo's
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexisrx96/physure/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CHANGELOG.md</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v023">
          <span class="release-version">v0.2.3</span>
          <span class="release-date">2026</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <h3>What's new</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>physure-java on Maven Central</strong>: JNI bindings published as <code>io.github.alexisrx96:physure-java</code>, bundling native libraries for Linux, macOS, and Windows (x86_64 + aarch64) in one jar — no <code>java.library.path</code> setup needed.</li>
          <li><strong>PHS plugin system</strong>: Native Rust plugins (ABI v2, hot-reload) and Python <code>.py</code> extension modules load through the same <code>use name[, name2] from &lt;domain&gt;</code> syntax. Scaffold one with <code>phs new-plugin myplugin --lang rust</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Domain-gated builtins</strong>: Core functions are always available; <code>calc</code>, <code>plot</code>, and <code>array</code> builtins now require an explicit <code>use ... from</code> import, keeping the base interpreter small.</li>
          <li><strong>physure-lsp improvements</strong>: Context-aware autocompletion for <code>use</code>/<code>from</code> statements, and native aarch64 Linux builds now ship alongside every <code>phs</code> release.</li>
          <li><strong>New docs</strong>: A dedicated <a href="/cli">PHS README</a> and a top-level <code>INSTALL.md</code> covering all four ways to use physure — the standalone <code>phs</code> CLI, the Python package, the Rust crate, and the Java bindings.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Install</h3>
        <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://physure.irvintorres.com/install.sh | sh  # phs CLI (macOS/Linux)
pip install physure                                          # Python package
cargo install physure --bin phs                              # Rust / cargo</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v022">
          <span class="release-version">v0.2.2</span>
          <span class="release-date">2026</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <h3>What's new</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Standalone Native Rust PHS Engine (<code>phs</code>)</strong>: Pure Rust interpreter executable with zero Python dependencies. Supports script execution (<code>phs file.phs</code>), CLI evaluation (<code>phs "500 N / 2 m^2 => kPa"</code>), and standalone terminal REPL.</li>
          <li><strong>VS Code Extension <code>vsc-physure 0.2.2</code></strong>: Launches native <code>phs</code> executable directly, native REPL terminal, <code>physure-lsp</code> high-performance Rust language server, shebang detection (<code>#!/usr/bin/env phs</code>), and <code>Physure: New PHS File</code> command.</li>
          <li><strong>PyO3 Native Bridge</strong>: Fast PyO3 FFI bridge (<code>physure._core.evaluate_phs_native</code>) exposing the Rust PHS evaluator directly to Python host applications.</li>
          <li><strong>Display Text & Multiline Blocks</strong>: Full support for verbatim Markdown display text blocks (<code>```...```</code>) and indented multiline function bodies in PHS grammar.</li>
          <li><strong>Vector & Uncertainty Builtins</strong>: Element-wise vector power and division (<code>Vector ^ 2</code>, <code>k * q / Vector^2</code>), <code>linspace</code>, <code>plot</code>, and uncertainty literal propagation (<code>+/-</code>).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Install</h3>
        <pre><code>cargo install --path physure-core --bin phs  # Native binary
pip install physure                           # Python package
code --install-extension vsc-physure-0.2.2.vsix # VS Code Extension</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v018">
          <span class="release-version">v0.1.8</span>
          <span class="release-date">2026</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <h3>What's new</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Chemistry extension added under <code>physure.ext.chemistry</code>: Species formula parsing, automatic stoichiometric reaction balancing, theoretical yields, kinetics, and thermodynamic free energy checks</li>
          <li>Native Symbolic Engine (<code>physure.domain.symbolic.native.Expr</code>): Zero-dependency algebraic simplification (<code>.simplify()</code>) and analytical differentiation (<code>.diff()</code>) with Rust core backend and pure-Python fallback</li>
          <li>Ahead-of-Time Physics Compiler (<code>physure.ext.compiler</code>): <code>compile_physics_model</code> traces and verifies formulas, baking them into high-speed unitless raw-float kernels</li>
          <li>Symbolic Regression (<code>physure.ext.symbolic_regression</code>): <code>SymbolicRegressor</code> fits physically valid formulas to empirical data using genetic programming</li>
          <li>Unit-Aware Plotting (<code>physure.plotting</code>): <code>plot()</code>, <code>plot_slices()</code>, and <code>plot_covariance()</code> with automatic axis labels, error bars, and covariance visualization</li>
          <li>Python 3.13 and 3.14 support added to CI matrix</li>
          <li>Improved startup performance — import budget tracked in CI</li>
          <li>GrammarInterpreter: added <code>= ?</code> query syntax for variable inspection</li>
          <li><code>physure repl</code> now accepts an inline expression argument</li>
          <li>CODATA 2022 constants updated — 400+ physical constants available</li>
          <li>Rust core (<code>physure-core</code>) updated for PyO3 0.22 compatibility</li>
          <li>Pandas integration improved: better dtype handling for quantity columns</li>
          <li>Test coverage gate raised to 80%+</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Install</h3>
        <pre><code>pip install physure==0.1.8</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v017">
          <span class="release-version">v0.1.7</span>
          <span class="release-date">2025</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <ul>
          <li>Added JAX backend with full <code>jax.jit</code> and <code>jax.vmap</code> support</li>
          <li>Functional API (<code>physure.functional</code>) for explicit covariance state in JAX pipelines</li>
          <li>FunctionalState registered as a JAX pytree</li>
          <li>Correlated uncertainty propagation via covariance store</li>
          <li><code>propagation_mode</code> context manager to switch modes</li>
          <li>Fixed temperature offset arithmetic (Celsius/Fahrenheit difference returns Kelvin delta)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v016">
          <span class="release-version">v0.1.6</span>
          <span class="release-date">2025</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <ul>
          <li>PyTorch backend: <code>__torch_dispatch__</code> integration for JIT compilation</li>
          <li>Unit checks at trace time → compiled graph sees only raw tensors (&lt;1.1× overhead)</li>
          <li>NumPy backend with Numba JIT support</li>
          <li>physure grammar interpreter (<code>physure.ext.grammar</code>)</li>
          <li>REPL and CLI entry point (<code>python -m physure</code>)</li>
          <li>SymPy integration for symbolic differentiation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v015">
          <span class="release-version">v0.1.5</span>
          <span class="release-date">2024</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <ul>
          <li>Rust core (<code>physure-core</code>) via PyO3: RationalUnit, UnitRegistry, CovarianceStore</li>
          <li>Zero-dependency base install — pure Python fallback for all Rust paths</li>
          <li>SI prefix support: all 24 prefixes (yocto to yotta)</li>
          <li>125+ named units across 28 dimensions</li>
          <li>CODATA physical constants module</li>
          <li>Pydantic v2 integration for quantity validation in models</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="release">
      <div class="release-header">
        <h2 id="v010">
          <span class="release-version">v0.1.0</span>
          <span class="release-date">2024</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="release-body">
        <p>Initial public release — "The Over-Engineered Homework Validator".</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Core <code>Quantity</code> class with unit-safe arithmetic</li>
          <li>Unit expression parser (/, *, ^, SI prefixes)</li>
          <li>Basic uncertainty propagation (independent errors)</li>
          <li>Temperature offset units (°C, °F, K)</li>
          <li>MIT License</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-info" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
    <p>For the full commit history, see the <a href="https://github.com/Alexisrx96/physure/commits/main" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub commits</a>. To report a bug or request a feature, <a href="https://github.com/Alexisrx96/physure/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue</a>.</p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .releases { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0; }

  .release {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border2);
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    position: relative;
  }
  .release::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -5px; top: 0.85rem;
    width: 8px; height: 8px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--border2);
  }
  .release.current::before { background: var(--accent2); }

  .release-header h2 {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;
    border: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .release-version { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; color: var(--text); }
  .release-date { color: var(--text3); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 400; font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; }
  .release-body h3 { color: var(--text3); font-size: 0.8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; margin: 1rem 0 0.4rem; }
  .release-body ul { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .release-body pre { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
</style>
